      "role": "user",
      "content": "{\"role\": \"C\", \"goal\": \"OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity\", \"experiment\": \"7071a734391848d0-h01\", \"ordinal\": 1, \"integration_source_ids\": [], \"current_source_paths\": [\"src/model-view/primetest.c\", \"src/sources/primetest.c\"], \"context_as_data\": {\"sources\": [], \"interactions\": [], \"automatic_observations\": {\"baseline\": {\"experiment_id\": \"baseline-e1\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.5, \"mean_seconds\": 0.044077253279586635}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}, \"selected_parent\": null, \"selected_base_commit\": \"e478abf2cda3d1d909c3c8faa4f14ac0aa283b7e\", \"recent_own_role\": [], \"own_role_history\": [], \"node_development\": {\"attempt\": 1, \"remaining_attempts\": 3, \"previous_attempts\": [], \"confirmation_data_available\": false}}, \"documents_as_data\": [], \"deadline\": 1792286004.143035, \"generation_deadline\": 1792286004.143035, \"protocol_id\": \"416b92e5c32775f0f1cf016fef585dea35535d58098fa0b5814f6a351ed4a5f9\", \"remaining_tokens\": 589824, \"official_statement_as_data\": \"Problem E\\nYet More Primes\\nPrime test is a common task in many applications, specially for security, cryptography,\\nand other interest areas. There are a number of techniques used to verify if a number is\\nprime, and the common problem is that all techniques consumes lots of time.\\nThe problem is easy to solve, giving the number to be tested and time enough to calculate.\\nBut a weird scientist took our numbers, and cut them in two halves, so we have ﬁrst to\\nre-arrange the numbers, looking for the primes. In this case, we have the certainty that all\\nthe numbers we have are primes, but the problem is how to re-order them\\nInput\\nIn the ﬁrst line, there is the numberP of prime numbers (1≤ P≤ 1000). Then, 2P lines\\nwith parts of the numbers to be tested, with no order. Prime numbers in this problem are\\ntaken as strings, so ﬁrst half and second half simply means an arbitrary cut of such string.\\nFor instance, prime number 504155039 can be cut in 5041 and 55039, or 504155 and 039\\nNote that second half numbers could begin with a lead zero, so you must take it into\\naccount. Of course, this doesn’t happen with the ﬁrst half.\\nThe input must be read from the standard input.\\nOutput\\nYou have to print the list of the P prime numbers, in ascendant order.\\nThe output must be written to the standard output.\\nExample\\nInput Output\\n4 1000213\\n50415 5041501\\n100 5575001\\n5041 504155039\\n55750 504155713\\n5039\\n01\\n0213\\n55713\\n\\n\\nThis experiment uses a reviewed finite certified input/output contract only. There are six private research cases and six distinct private confirmation cases. P is in {1,2,4,8,16,30}; the input lists two separately permuted groups of P decimal fragments. Left fragments have no leading zero and end in an even digit; right fragments have seven characters including a leading zero. Each original pair comes from a true prime and the multiset of every admissible prime concatenation equals the P source occurrences. Concatenations are at most 600971707; output is compared as exact bytes. The official example has an unresolved P/output-count contradiction. No claim is made for the whole contest domain or arbitrary fragment orders. The fixed benchmark is one member of the same certified finite set; no hidden values or expected outputs are supplied. Target: Linux ARM64, four CPU cores, 16 GiB RAM, zero GPUs. Compilation: GCC -O2 -std=c11 -Werror=uninitialized -Werror=maybe-uninitialized -lm.\", \"runtime_as_data\": {\"cpu_cores\": 4, \"OMP_NUM_THREADS\": 4, \"architecture\": \"ARM64\", \"candidate_GPU\": false, \"comparison\": \"Exact output bytes against official reference\"}, \"mother_model_as_data\": {\"phase\": \"preparation\", \"status\": \"complete\", \"guidance\": \"Research candidate construction and batch filtering. Parse each fragment once and construct candidates as left × 10^right_length + right; preserve textual length despite leading zeros. The certified right length is seven. Compare arithmetic construction alone against adding small-prime residue filters: precompute left contribution and right residues, then reject only combinations divisible by a tested prime and unequal to that prime. Explore compact per-left bitmasks for the at most 30 right indices, but retain the simpler loop if mask construction costs more. Every survivor must receive an exact primality test. Keep that kernel fixed for controlled comparisons. Enumerate every pair occurrence, retain duplicate results, and provide separable pipeline changes for integration.\", \"analysis\": \"The only supplied performance evidence is a correct baseline with mean 0.0440772533 seconds and score 0.5; no usable profiling or optimized-parent evidence is available. Repeated floating-point fmod across successive divisors is the strongest source-based bottleneck hypothesis, not a measured attribution. The certified contract permits at most 900 cross-group candidates, values no greater than 600971707, and exactly P prime occurrences. Consequently, bounded exact primality algorithms and eliminating repeated string conversion are appropriate research directions; threading and sorting are lower priorities. All trees must preserve correctness throughout the certified contract, including duplicate occurrences and leading-zero widths. Contract bounds may guide algorithms, but hidden values and expected outputs must never be substituted for computation. No broader contest-domain claim should rely on the contradictory example.\", \"origin\": \"model output from this fresh run; no operator analysis\"}}, \"required_json_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"hypothesis\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"transformations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"sources_kept\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"uniqueItems\": true, \"maxItems\": 0}, \"files\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"content\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\", \"content\"]}}, \"notes\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"hypothesis\", \"transformations\", \"sources_kept\", \"files\", \"notes\"]}}\n\nCURRENT SOURCE AS DATA:\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <math.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar strToTest[MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{ long int root;\n  long int factor=2;\n  int prime=1;\n  root = sqrtl(value);\n  while ((factor<=root) && (prime))\n        { prime = fmod((double)value,(double) factor) > 0.0;\n\t\t  factor++;\n\t    }\n  return prime;\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n\t     { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while(primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n\t\t      { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0;\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n\t  for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n\t      { strcpy(strToTest,firstHalf[i]);\n\t        strcat(strToTest,secondHalf[j]);\n\t\t\tprimeToTest=atol(strToTest);\n\t\t\tif (isprime(primeToTest)) \n\t\t\t   { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n\t\t\t   }\n\t      }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\t  \n  return 0;\n}\n--- END FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <math.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar strToTest[MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{ long int root;\n  long int factor=2;\n  int prime=1;\n  root = sqrtl(value);\n  while ((factor<=root) && (prime))\n        { prime = fmod((double)value,(double) factor) > 0.0;\n\t\t  factor++;\n\t    }\n  return prime;\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n\t     { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while(primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n\t\t      { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0;\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n\t  for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n\t      { strcpy(strToTest,firstHalf[i]);\n\t        strcat(strToTest,secondHalf[j]);\n\t\t\tprimeToTest=atol(strToTest);\n\t\t\tif (isprime(primeToTest)) \n\t\t\t   { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n\t\t\t   }\n\t      }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\t  \n  return 0;\n}\n--- END FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n"
